   },
   {
    "text": "President of ACADIA, 2018–2020",
    "layer": "Fact",
    "path": "Spread rv-spread/Portrait column rv-sticky rv-no-reveal/Key facts rv-facts"
   },
   {
    "text": "Associate Dean for Research and Creative Practice, Taubman College",
    "layer": "Fact",
    "path": "Spread rv-spread/Portrait column rv-sticky rv-no-reveal/Key facts rv-facts"
   },
   {
    "text": "Kathy Velikov, FAIA",
    "layer": "Name",
    "path": "Directors spreads/Spread rv-spread/Text column"
   },
   {
    "text": "Director, RVTR. Professor of Architecture and Associate Dean for Research and Creative Practice, Taubman College",
    "layer": "Role",
    "path": "Directors spreads/Spread rv-spread/Text column"
   },
   {
    "text": "Kathy Velikov is an architect, researcher and educator, and a director of RVTR, the research lab she co-founded in Toronto in 2007 with Geoffrey Thün, Colin Ripley and Paul Raff. She is Professor of Architecture and Associate Dean for Research and Creative Practice at Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning, University of Michigan, and directs the Institute for Advanced Construction Futures, a University of Michigan Impact Institute funded in January 2026 to advance low-carbon construction. She is licensed in Michigan and Ontario. In 2025 she was elevated to the College of Fellows of the American Institute of Architects.",
    "layer": "",
    "path": "Directors spreads/Spread rv-spread/Text column"
   },
   {
    "text": "Taubman College profile",
    "link": "https://taubmancollege.umich.edu/faculty/directory/kathy-velikov/",
    "layer": "Profile rv-link",
    "path": "Directors spreads/Spread rv-spread/Text column"
   },
   {
    "text": "Support",
    "layer": "Text",
    "path": "Container/Title Container/Label"
   },
   {
    "text": "Funding and collaboration",
    "layer": "Title",
    "path": "Awards/Container/Title Container"
   },
   {
    "text": "RVTR is funded by the University of Michigan and by external grants and sponsored research. Funders have included the National Science Foundation, the National Fish and Wildlife Foundation, the U.S. Department of Energy through the National Renewable Energy Laboratory, the U.S. Department of Transportation, the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, the AIA Upjohn Research Initiative, Bloomberg Philanthropies, the Mellon Foundation through the Michigan-Mellon project, the Belmont Forum’s SUGI Food-Water-Energy Nexus program, the Urban Studies Foundation, Ford Motor Company, Guardian Industries, Wanxiang Group, the Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council of Canada, the Canada Council for the Arts, the National Research Council of Canada and the Ontario Power Authority. The lab’s collaborators include engineers, scientists, archaeologists, landscape architects, fabricators and other research groups, among them the Kelsey Museum of Archaeology, Arup, OPSYS, POST, TU Delft, Purdue University, Michigan State University and Wayne State University.",
